CORE
main.cpp
--cpp17 --no-unwinding-assertions --no-body-assertions
no body for callee mutate
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
--
SOUNDNESS: a call to a function with no available body is modelled as
"assign nondet to the lhs, change nothing else", so `x == 1` after
`mutate(&x)` is "proved".  The dedicated `--no-body-assertions` option
(added for this test) emits the failing `no-body` property regardless of
the unwinding-assertions setting, so any test that bounds loops with
--no-unwinding-assertions can still demand a diagnosis for missing
bodies.  Measured before landing: making the property UNCONDITIONAL
breaks exactly two intentional-stub tests (String_Abstraction15's
declared-only malloc under --string-abstraction, and
cpp11_list_pushback_libcxx via an object-bits blowup), so the default
stays opt-in.  The deeper resolution -- havocking the state reachable
through the callee -- remains future work; the assertion at least makes
the vacuity visible.
Found while diagnosing libcxx23_vector_pushback (silently bodyless
libc++ member) and confirmed again by gcc16_optional_transform's
silently dropped _Storage constructor (round 109/110).
